The distribution obligations are met by the repository's `NOTICE`, which
`vite.config.ts` copies into `dist/index.html` together with the EPL-2.0 text:
that bundle is committed and embedded in the binary, so it ships elkjs in object
form.

The EPL does not extend to `elk.ts` or to anything else here. A file that only
imports elkjs is not a Modified Work under EPL-2.0 §1, which excludes files that
exist "solely ... to link to, bind by name, or subclass the Program". Keeping
elkjs behind `layout.ts` is what makes the engine replaceable: `layout.ts` states
the contract with no reference to elkjs, and `operatorView.ts` takes a
`GraphLayout` rather than constructing one, so `elk.ts` is imported once outside
this directory, where `main.ts` mounts the pane. Replacing the engine replaces
`elk.ts` and that one import.
